# Both functions have a guard that a parsed record fails, and a slow version with a loop that holds no counter. The loop of joins() joins texts by a template, that of seeks() asks for equality: the walked form of either is cheaper than the
# straight one (a join 146 instructions a turn, an equality 13), so both slow versions are walked, as they would be with a counter beside: what a loop is compiled to does not hang on an `n++` that has nothing to do with it.
# f1 = joins, f2 = seeks
